WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.699 --> 00:00:19.919
[United States Navy Training Film. Nonclassified.
Copyright 1945 by Hugh Harman Productions,

00:00:19.919 --> 00:00:20.169
Inc.]

00:00:20.019 --> 00:00:20.329
[Commandments For Health]

00:00:20.329 --> 00:00:20.579
[Drinking Water]

00:00:20.539 --> 00:00:22.320
Lets consider water.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:27.379
Not carbonated water or toilet water, but
just plain everyday water. H2O.

00:00:27.379 --> 00:00:32.610
You probably never gave it a second thought.
It used to be when if you wanted a drink of

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:34.320
water, you just took it. But now ...

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:38.620
[Third Commandment Drinking Water] "Thou shalt
not drink water from any other source than

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:45.589
that designated, else thou become victim to
an unhappy fate more painful than Japanese

00:00:45.589 --> 00:00:46.079
lead."

00:00:46.079 --> 00:00:53.079
"Thou shalt use thy water sparingly and wisely
else thy days and thy brothers days shall

00:00:53.479 --> 00:00:56.000
be numbered."

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:00.729
So Brother, in a combat area get your water
from a Lister bag.

00:01:00.729 --> 00:01:05.759
It may not taste like the water from that
old oaken bucket, but it is pure and healthful.

00:01:05.759 --> 00:01:07.950
Use it wisely!

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:14.060
When you're on a march under a blistering
hot sun, even that chlorine tastes good.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:17.070
So good, that some jerks want to down it all
at once!

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:19.840
Like, eh, like our old friend, McGillicuddy.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:21.939
Kinda hot ain't it, Mac?

00:01:21.939 --> 00:01:24.460
[A tired looking solider nods slowly]

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:26.869
Well, why don't you take a drink?

00:01:26.869 --> 00:01:33.869
[Gradually starts drinking larger portions
of water]

00:01:37.159 --> 00:01:37.670
Here we go.

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:40.380
[Pours water over his head and into his pants
and soaks his feet in his water-filled hat]

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:43.799
Hmm. See what I mean? What a jerk.

00:01:43.799 --> 00:01:49.600
For two months we've been begging him to take
a bath and now he does it.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.679
[Marches proudly upright]

00:01:53.679 --> 00:02:00.679
The Great McGillicuddy. Full of him and full
of water. An empty canteen and an empty head.

00:02:01.799 --> 00:02:04.840
[A blistering hot sun blazes above as he marches]

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.499
Now let's see how far he gets.

00:02:07.499 --> 00:02:12.660
[Begins to drag himself slowly]

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:16.180
Oh, beginning to drag it, eh?

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:20.760
[Is laying on a desert floor]

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:26.980
Now it's really getting hot! Hotter than it
was before, huh, Mac?

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:32.069
Oh, your tongue's dry isn't it, Mac? You know
what you ought to have?

00:02:32.069 --> 00:02:39.069
You ought to have a nice, cool drink of water.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:43.049
Why don't you look in your canteen? You started
out with plenty of water.

00:02:43.049 --> 00:02:50.049
[Tries to suck water out of his empty canteen
but only a drop comes out]

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:55.730
There it is, Mac! Water! Get it, Mac!

00:02:55.730 --> 00:02:59.079
[Manages to catch the drop on his tongue but
it evaporates]

00:02:59.079 --> 00:03:01.439
Aw, that's a dirty low-down shame

00:03:01.439 --> 00:03:03.129
Water...water...

00:03:03.129 --> 00:03:10.129
All right, Mac. Don't get dramatic. After
all, you're only a couple of miles away from

00:03:11.469 --> 00:03:12.110
camp.

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:17.260
[Spots an oasis nearby and runs towards it]

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:17.900
Water...water!

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:24.900
Mac. Mac, come back! Mac! Don't, Mac! Come
back! Come back here! STOP!

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:31.430
Listen, knucklehead. You can't drink that
water! It hasn't been inspected - it might

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:32.049
even be poison!

00:03:32.049 --> 00:03:32.719
Yetta, yetta, yetta! Quit beating your gums.

00:03:32.719 --> 00:03:33.299
[Dives into water and drinks it]

00:03:33.299 --> 00:03:40.299
[Across the water a gorilla drinks the same
water, and afterwards collapses]

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:09.269
Now how do you feel about drinking that water?
Not so good, huh?

00:04:09.269 --> 00:04:12.499
Let's take a look upstream and see what gives
that stuff its kick.

00:04:12.499 --> 00:04:15.329
[Aerial view shows objects in the water upstream]

00:04:15.329 --> 00:04:22.329
See, Mac? Here's you, here's the gorilla,
a few dead Japs, some pigs, and a native village.

00:04:25.889 --> 00:04:26.950
What's wrong with the native village?

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:30.360
[Scrolls across an image of the village and
shows a head]

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:35.200
Very pretty, very pretty, but do you know
what that is?

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:40.190
That, McGillicuddy, is a head hunter's head.

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:43.940
[Toilet flushing]

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:47.640
Direct from the manufacturer to the consumer!
A prize in every package!

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:48.070
[Stomach is full of weird germs swimming around]

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:49.390
And brother, you've hit the jackpot.

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:56.390
[Releases gas and is rushed past a sign that
says 'To The Head']

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:23.510
[Immediately returns to the head while releasing
gas multiple times]

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:27.870
Monotonous isn't it, McGillicuddy?

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:27.880
[The End. MN 2808 c. 1945]

